explanation:
Line 11 starts one child task per amount, and every child mutates the same `Tally` object. Child tasks run in parallel, so two of them can read and write `total` at the same moment and lose an update. Because `Tally` isn't `Sendable`, Swift 6 rejects the capture ("passing closure as a 'sending' parameter risks causing data races", or a main-actor isolation error when your project uses main-actor default isolation).

Let each child return its value and sum in the parent, which only ever runs one loop iteration at a time:

```swift
func totalOfAll(_ amounts: [Decimal]) async -> Decimal {
    await withTaskGroup(of: Decimal.self) { group in
        for amount in amounts {
            group.addTask { amount }
        }
        var total: Decimal = 0
        for await part in group {
            total += part
        }
        return total
    }
}
```

If the state really must be shared, make `Tally` an `actor` and call `await tally.add(amount)`.
